  "title": "Data Synchronization Strategies",
  "description": "Implement data synchronization with Change Data Capture.",
  "order": 6,
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedMinutes": 18,
  "tags": ["data-sync", "cdc", "replication"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Data synchronization keeps data consistent across services. Change Data Capture (CDC) is a powerful technique for propagating database changes."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Key Concepts"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "Change Data Capture (CDC)",
            "definition": "Capturing database changes and streaming them to other systems."
          },
          {
            "term": "Event-Driven Sync",
            "definition": "Synchronizing data via events."
          },
          {
            "term": "Batch Sync",
            "definition": "Synchronizing data in batches at intervals."
          },
          {
            "term": "Dual Write",
            "definition": "Writing to multiple databases in one transaction (risky)."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Synchronization Strategies"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": ["Strategy", "Latency", "Complexity", "Use Case"],
        "rows": [
          ["CDC", "Near real-time", "Medium", "Replication, search index"],
          ["Event-driven", "Real-time", "Medium", "Cross-service updates"],
          ["Batch", "Minutes/hours", "Low", "Analytics, reporting"],
          ["Dual write", "Real-time", "High (risky)", "Avoid if possible"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Change Data Capture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "CDC captures database changes (inserts, updates, deletes) from the transaction log and streams them. Tools like Debezium read the log and publish changes to Kafka."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "CDC Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Source database with transaction log",
          "CDC connector (Debezium) reads the log",
          "Changes published to Kafka",
          "Consumers update read models or other databases"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Avoid Dual Writes"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Dual writes (writing to two databases in one transaction) are risky. If one write fails, data becomes inconsistent. Use CDC or events instead."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "msa-cdc",
        "caption": "Change Data Capture Flow",
        "definition": "flowchart TD\n    DB[(Source DB)] -->|Transaction Log| CDC[CDC Connector]\n    CDC -->|Change Events| Kafka[(Kafka)]\n    Kafka --> Consumer1[Read Model]\n    Kafka --> Consumer2[Search Index]\n    Kafka --> Consumer3[Cache Invalidation]"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
